WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:04.010
Abraham: As we’ve had more time on
our hands during lockdown many more

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.060
of us have been using technology
to entertain ourselves, as well

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.800
as to continue our work remotely.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.180
Children have been remote learning often
sharing devices with others in a family.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:17.199
Mobile devices are commonly used by
everyone including those who have not

00:00:17.199 --> 00:00:21.630
used technology before, such as the
elderly who now regularly 'facetime'

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.740
or 'chat' to family all over the world.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.639
We are all using our mobile phones
more to order food, book appointments,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:34.019
organise parcel deliveries, do our
banking, purchasing goods and book

00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:35.709
medical tests and appointments.

00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:38.980
This has opened us all
up to Phishing attacks.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.000
Nicole: Covid19 has itself
helped to increase the level of

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.690
phishing attacks on individuals.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.919
Any kind of change within the world,
produces an opportunity for criminals

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.800
to exploit people’s confusion.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:56.230
As we search for more vaccine and
Covid19 testing information, vaccine

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.155
and test suppliers are becoming
targets of phishing attacks as are the

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.390
individuals who seek their services.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.940
Bogus emails and SMS messages
about their services can affect

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.580
organisations and individuals alike.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.510
This is also impacted by remote
working and the use of home networks.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.960
Abraham: Phishing covers a range
of different social engineering

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.729
attacks which trick victims into
sharing sensitive information such

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:26.119
as passwords, usernames, and credit
card details for malicious reasons.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:30.509
They can be random or more sophisticated
and targeted known as spear phishing.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:35.300
This involves malicious emails being
sent to a well-researched victim.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.589
Other forms of phishing like 'vishing'
or voice phishing and 'smishing' or

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:46.089
SMS phishing involve the use of mobile
phones and phone calls or SMS messaging.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.509
Victims are contacted by what appears
to be trusted companies, banks or

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.990
organisations and told they have
to act immediately to organise

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.940
something or respond to something
by giving their private details.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.539
Nicole: Most of us have had a message from
a parcel delivery company or some other

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:05.919
organisation asking us to click on a link.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:11.000
Most of us know that this is bogus,
but individuals can be caught out

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:14.560
if they are expecting something to
be delivered or are too trusting!

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.579
A common example of vishing is when
an employee would be called by someone

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.809
posing as ‘IT Helpdesk’ asking them
to install something on their computer

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.890
which turns out to be malware.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:30.870
A common example of smishing is a
text pretending to be ‘Royal Mail’

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.920
saying there is an import charge for
a fake parcel that requires payment.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.160
Abraham: Other common threats come
from 'water holing' a computer attack

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.110
strategy where an attacker guesses or
observes which websites an individual

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:47.150
or organisation often uses, and
they then affect them with malware.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:51.520
Identity theft and 'synthetic identities'
are becoming more commonplace with

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.960
the potential to affect us all.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.589
These involve creating fabricated
credentials that are not

00:02:56.599 --> 00:02:58.230
associated with the real person.

